.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd October 13, 2026
.Dt FSCONF_MKDB 8
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm fsconf_mkdb
.Nd compile the file system table for the kernel
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl f Ar fstab
.Op Fl o Ar fsconf
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The kernel has to know which device holds the root file system before it can
read any file system, so it can't read
.Pa /etc/fstab .
.Nm
compiles
.Pa /etc/fstab
into
.Pa /boot/fsconf ,
the binary form described in
.Xr fsconf 5 ,
which the boot loader loads beside the kernel.
Run it after changing the
.Pa /
line of
.Pa /etc/fstab .
.Pp
The new file is written beside the old one and renamed over it, so the boot
loader never finds half of it.
It must hold an entry for
.Pa / .
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl f Ar fstab
Read
.Ar fstab
instead of
.Pa /etc/fstab .
.It Fl o Ar fsconf
Write
.Ar fsconf
instead of
.Pa /boot/fsconf .
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /boot/fsconf -compact
.It Pa /etc/fstab
The file system table.
.It Pa /boot/fsconf
Its compiled form.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr fsconf 5 ,
.Xr fstab 5 ,
.Xr mount 8 ,
.Xr pwd_mkdb 8
.Sh CAVEATS
The boot loader loads
.Pa /boot/fsconf
from the boot partition.
Where that isn't the root file system, copy the new file there too.
